     1	// vaultFresh's answer (V9): is the newest COMPLETE export in the vault bucket at most 7 days 18 hours old? An uptime
     2	// check calls it every 15 minutes; failing for 6 hours emails Christie, so the email lands at about 8 days. Pure: the
     3	// bucket and the clock are passed in.
     4	//
     5	//   200 {case: "fresh"} — the newest complete export is at most 7 d 18 h old;
     6	//   503 {case: "stale"} — it's older;  503 {case: "none"} — there's no complete export at all;
     7	//   500 {case: "error"} — anything else (the handler catches it), e.g. vault-watch@ can no longer list the bucket.
     8	// The body holds only the case, the folder name and its age: never member data (there is none in a listing anyway).
     9	'use strict';
    10	
    11	const C = require('./vault-config');
    12	const { listFolders, completeMetadata } = require('./vault-bucket');
    13	const { makeLimiter, limitedBucket, realTimeLimit } = require('./deadline');
    14	
    15	async function checkFreshness({ bucket, now = Date.now }) {
    16	  let newest = null;
    17	  for (const folder of await listFolders(bucket, C.WEEKLY, C.FOLDER_CAP_ALL)) {
    18	    const meta = await completeMetadata(bucket, folder);
    19	    if (!meta) continue;
    20	    const created = Date.parse(meta.metadata && meta.metadata.timeCreated);
    21	    if (Number.isNaN(created)) throw new Error(`${meta.name}: the listing gave no timeCreated`);
    22	    if (!newest || created > newest.created) newest = { folder, created };
    23	  }
    24	  if (!newest) return { status: 503, body: { case: 'none', folder: null, ageHours: null } };
    25	  const ageMs = now() - newest.created;
    26	  const body = { folder: newest.folder, ageHours: Math.round(ageMs / 360000) / 10 };
    27	  return ageMs <= C.FRESH_MS ? { status: 200, body: { case: 'fresh', ...body } } : { status: 503, body: { case: 'stale', ...body } };
    28	}
    29	
    30	// The handler's answer: checkFreshness, with any error (a listing refused or too slow, a bucket the client can't build)
    31	// as a 500 whose body says only "error"; the reason goes to the log. Every listing is held to FRESH_BUDGET_MS in all,
    32	// so the answer always comes before the function's 30 s timeout (impl review round 1).
    33	async function answerFreshness({ bucket, now = Date.now, timeLimit = realTimeLimit, logError = () => {} }) {
    34	  try {
    35	    const limit = makeLimiter({ deadline: now() + C.FRESH_BUDGET_MS, now, timeLimit });
    36	    return await checkFreshness({ bucket: limitedBucket(typeof bucket === 'function' ? bucket() : bucket, limit), now });
    37	  } catch (e) {
    38	    logError(`vaultFresh: ${e.message}`);
    39	    return { status: 500, body: { case: 'error', folder: null, ageHours: null } };
    40	  }
    41	}
    42	
    43	module.exports = { checkFreshness, answerFreshness };
     1	# My Clay Hub: restoring data from the vault (`my-clay-hub` project)
     2	
     3	The runbook for getting data back **from the weekly off-project copy** (plan `my-clay-hub-d4-vault-export`, V14).
     4	Restoring is a change to production data: nothing here runs without Christie's explicit go-ahead for that restore.
     5	Claude never runs an import; Christie does every step in the Console (there's no gcloud on this laptop).
     6	
     7	## Which backup to use first
     8	
     9	`my-clay-hub` has four kinds of backup (DECISIONS #44). Use the nearest one that has what you need:
    10	
    11	| Backup | Where | Kept | Use it when |
    12	|---|---|---|---|
    13	| Point-in-time recovery | inside `my-clay-hub` | 7 days, to the minute | a recent mistake: read or restore the database as it was at a moment this week |
    14	| Managed daily backup | inside `my-clay-hub` | 98 days | an older mistake, while the project itself is fine |
    15	| Delete protection | inside `my-clay-hub` | — | stops the database itself being deleted |
    16	| **The vault export** | bucket `my-clay-hub-vault-exports` in **`tinker-hq-vault`** | 56 days (about 8 weekly copies), plus 7 days of soft delete | the project's own backups are gone or can't be trusted (a deleted or compromised project) |
    17	
    18	The vault is the last resort, because it lives outside the project that could fail.
    19	
    20	## 1. Choose the folder
    21	
    22	In `tinker-hq-vault` → Cloud Storage → `my-clay-hub-vault-exports` → `weekly/`, each export is a folder named
    23	`<YYYY-MM-DD>-<6 characters>/`, the UTC date of the Sunday it was made (or of a manual "Force run").
    24	
    25	**Use only a complete folder.** A folder is complete when it holds a file named after the folder itself:
    26	`weekly/2026-10-04-ab12cd/2026-10-04-ab12cd.overall_export_metadata`. The 56-day lifecycle deletes a folder's files
    27	gradually (over up to a day), so an old folder can be half there. A folder without that file is never used.
    28	
    29	Pick the newest complete folder from **before** the problem began. Two folders on the same date are both complete
    30	copies (a rare duplicate, by design). Either one will do; take the later one.
    31	
    32	## 2. Decide where it goes: a scratch database, or production
    33	
    34	**An import overwrites documents that have the same path and deletes nothing.** Into a database that already holds
    35	data, it's a merge, not a rollback: documents created after the export stay, and documents deleted since come back.
    36	
    37	- **Rehearsals, and "what did it look like?"**: import into a **new, empty** database in `my-clay-hub` (for example
    38	  `restore-2026-10-04`), never `(default)`. Nothing the app reads changes. Create it in **production mode**, so its
    39	  rules deny every read and write from the start, and check its Rules tab says so before importing: it will hold
    40	  real member data, and this repo's `firestore.rules` covers `(default)` only. Delete the scratch database afterwards.
    41	- **A real restore into `(default)`**: first make a **new, verified** copy of the database as it is now, so the
    42	  restore itself can be undone. Don't use a Force run of `vaultExport` for this: if today's UTC date already has a
    43	  complete folder it correctly does nothing ("already done"). Instead, in `my-clay-hub` → Firestore → `(default)` →
    44	  Import/Export → **Export**: the entire database, to a folder name never used before,
    45	  `gs://my-clay-hub-vault-exports/manual/pre-restore-<YYYY-MM-DD>-<HHMM UTC>` (an export can't reuse a folder). Wait
    46	  until **that export's own operation** shows as finished successfully on the Import/Export page, then check that its
    47	  new folder holds its own `.overall_export_metadata` file (named after that folder). Only then go on.
    48	  (Point-in-time recovery also keeps the pre-restore state readable for 7 days, but it isn't a copy you can check.)
    49	  Then decide with Christie what "restore" means: a merge (import over the top), or a true rollback (clearing the
    50	  affected collections first, which is a bulk delete: snapshot, approval and the delete rules in the global CLAUDE.md
    51	  all apply).
    52	
    53	## 3. The temporary read grant (tinker-hq-vault)
    54	
    55	The import is done by the **importing database's Firestore service agent**, which needs to read the vault bucket.
    56	For `my-clay-hub` that's `service-760301318440@gcp-sa-firestore.iam.gserviceaccount.com` (shown on Firestore →
    57	Import/Export as "Import/Export jobs run as"). It already holds `firestoreExportWriter` on the vault bucket, for the
    58	weekly export, but that role can't read files.
    59	
    60	1. **Before:** screenshots of `tinker-hq-vault-backups` (Configuration and Permissions) and of `tinker-hq-vault`'s
    61	   IAM page, with Google-provided grants shown, exactly as in V-1.
    62	2. `tinker-hq-vault` → IAM → Roles: if it doesn't exist yet, create **`vaultImportReader`** with exactly
    63	   `storage.buckets.get` and `storage.objects.get`.
    64	3. `my-clay-hub-vault-exports` → Permissions → Grant access: `vaultImportReader` to the service agent above.
    65	   **On the bucket only, never on the project.**
    66	4. Wait a few minutes for the grant to take effect.
    67	
    68	## 4. Import
    69	
    70	The person importing needs `datastore.databases.import`. Only Christie (Owner) holds it, and no service account does.
    71	
    72	1. `my-clay-hub` → Firestore → choose the target database (step 2) → Import/Export → **Import**.
    73	2. Browse to the chosen folder and select its `.overall_export_metadata` file.
    74	3. Start the import. It runs as an operation: watch it on the Import/Export page until it finishes, and keep a
    75	   screenshot of the result.
    76	4. Check the result: the collections and a few known documents are there, with the values expected for that date.
    77	
    78	## 5. Remove the grant
    79	
    80	1. Remove `vaultImportReader` from the service agent on `my-clay-hub-vault-exports`. Leave the role defined; it's
    81	   harmless unused.
    82	2. **After:** the same screenshots as step 3.1. The staff bucket and the project IAM must match the "before" readings
    83	   exactly. The vault bucket's Permissions are back to V-1's two `vaultLister` grants plus the agent's `firestoreExportWriter`.
    84	3. Record it in `DECISIONS.md` (date, folder, target database, what was restored, the before/after check).
    85	
    86	## If something goes wrong
    87	
    88	- **The import is refused for permission:** the read grant (step 3) is missing or hasn't taken effect yet. Wait a few
    89	  minutes and try once more. If it's still refused, **stop**: record the exact denied permission and principal, and
    90	  change nothing until Christie approves that specific widening (on the bucket only), which then goes in the record.
    91	- **No complete folder in the vault:** the freshness alert should already have emailed (V9). Fall back to the in-project
    92	  backups, and treat the missing vault copy as its own incident (FUNCTIONS-ROLLBACK.md §9 for the job).
    93	- **Export files were deleted:** soft delete keeps deleted objects for 7 days. In `tinker-hq-vault` → Cloud Storage →
    94	  the bucket, show soft-deleted objects and restore the folder first. After 7 days a deletion is permanent (V12).
    95	
    96	The first restore rehearsal happens in Phase E, once real member data exists (Christie, O3), into a scratch database.
   190	- **Stopping a scheduled function: PAUSE its job** (Cloud Scheduler → the job → Pause). It is immediate and
   191	  reversible (Resume), and changes nothing the CLI reads, so a deploy still verifies. The Scheduler reading does see it:
   192	  a PAUSED job makes `scheduler_attested=no` until it's resumed and read again. The guard never deletes a function or a
   193	  job. Pause first whenever a Scheduler reading comes back `no`, then diagnose.
   194	- **Partial states** (the CLI creates or patches the job *after* the function):
   195	  - the function is live but its job is missing (the job's creation failed; the deploy exits non-zero): fix the cause,
   196	    then re-run the same sha under the same approval — the job is created then;
   197	  - the job kept its **old** schedule or retry settings (the function updated, the job's patch failed): the Scheduler
   198	    reading shows it (`no`); pause the job, then re-run the same sha;
   199	  - a schedule **removed from code** leaves its job behind, still calling the function. The deploy itself can't see
   200	    the job; the next Scheduler reading does (an extra job in the complete list → `scheduler_attested=no`). So before
   201	    such a change, pause the job, and ask Christie: deleting a job has no guarded path.
   202	- **The header exception (Christie, Oct 1, O6):** a scheduled function's HTTP response is written by firebase-functions
   203	  itself, so it cannot carry `x-tinker-reached`. The unauthenticated probe still runs on its `uri` and must be refused
   204	  (a public scheduled function would also start a real run). Nothing in the code can make a schedule public: the
   205	  pinned CLI sets its `run.invoker` to the runtime account (J3), and the probe and the IAM reading check the result.
   206	
   207	## 10. The vault codebase (D-4)
   208	
   209	`vault/vaultExport` copies the whole database to `gs://my-clay-hub-vault-exports/weekly/<date>-<6 chars>/` in
   210	`tinker-hq-vault` every Sunday 09:00 UTC, and waits for Google to finish. `vault/vaultFresh` answers an uptime check: 200
   211	while the newest complete copy is at most 7 days 18 hours old. Plan: `~/tinker-ai-configs/thoughts/plans/my-clay-hub-d4-vault-export.html`.
   212	
   213	- **To stop exports at once:** Cloud Scheduler → `firebase-schedule-vaultExport-us-central1` → **Pause** (§9). Never
   214	  delete the function or the job by hand. Pausing means the freshness alert fires in about 8 days; that's expected.
   215	- **A failure email** (log-based alert on `vaultexport` errors, V13): read the function's log. Its first line holds the
   216	  run's scheduleTime and key (the date); the error names the operation and the prefix. Scheduler retries three times
   217	  (about 10, 20, 40 minutes apart). A retry never starts a second export while the first is visible: it finds the
   218	  complete folder ("already done") or waits on the running export ("resume").
   219	- **A freshness email** (V9): `vaultFresh`'s body says `stale`, `none` or `error`. `error` usually means `vault-watch@`
   220	  can't list the bucket (its `vaultLister` grant on the bucket).
   221	- **Two grants live in `tinker-hq-vault`, on the vault bucket only:** `vaultLister` (to `vault-export@` and
   222	  `vault-watch@`, V-1) and `firestoreExportWriter` (to the Firestore service agent, V-4). `--attest` reads only
   223	  `my-clay-hub`'s IAM, so their record is V-1/V-4's screenshots and the exports themselves.
   224	- **Restoring from the vault:** `docs/my-clay-hub/DATA-RESTORE.md`.

